## G4, Argo CD : intégrité des sources
--- argocd-integrity.md, l. 1 à 29
   1 | # Overview
   2 | 
   3 | Argo CD permits declaring criteria for application sources integrity that, when not met, will prevent an application from syncing with a `ResourceComparison` error.
   4 | This is useful to verify the sources have not been tampered with by an unauthorized contributor.
   5 | 
   6 | Each Application Project can have its criteria configured in `AppProject`'s `.spec.sourceIntegrity`.
   7 | The criteria distinguish a type of verification they perform, and to which sources they apply.
   8 | 
   9 | Each application can be a subject of multiple checks, and the sync will be enabled only when all criteria are met.
  10 | 
  11 | > [!NOTE]
  12 | > Source Integrity Verification is only configured through `AppProject` manifests or CLI. UI is not supported yet.
  13 | 
  14 | > [!NOTE]
  15 | > Signature verification is not supported for the Application Sets populated by the git generator when they have the `project` field templated.
  16 | 
  17 | > [!WARNING]
  18 | > If source integrity is enforced, you will not be able to sync from local sources (i.e. `argocd app sync --local`) anymore.
  19 | 
  20 | ## Supported methods
  21 | 
  22 | - [Git GnuPG verification](./source-integrity-git-gpg.md) verifies that Git commits are GnuPG Signed. This is a modern method of the commit signature verification originally configured in `AppProjects`'s `signatureKeys`.
  23 | 
  24 | ## Multi-source applications
  25 | 
  26 | Each individual application source can be a subject of a different set of source integrity criteria, if desirable.
  27 | This is necessary if the sources are of a different type, such as Git and Helm.
  28 | But even different repositories of the same type can utilize different methods of verification, or their different configurations.
  29 | This is useful when an application combines sources maintained by different groups of people, or according to different contribution (and signing) guidelines.
